[{:seon.task/name "type-findings-are-an-evals-warnings"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: Jolt checks the success types of calls (``jolt.passes.types/check-form``, non-strict by default). It takes a unit first: `(jolt.passes.types/check-form (jolt.passes.types/new-unit) node)`; with the node alone it throws an arity error. Ran 2026-11-06 at a root and in an agent, on nodes from `seon.program/tap`: `(first 42)` answers `[{:op \"first\" :argpos 0 :type \"a number\" :pos {:line 1 :column 1} :msg \"`first` requires a seqable, but argument 1 is a number\"}]` (:op is a string), `(defn f [] (first 42))` the same with the inner call's :pos, and `(+ 1 2)` and `(defn g [x] (inc x))` answer `[]`. :pos is a position in the request's text (a call on line 4 of the request says :line 4), where an error's :seon.error/at is in the form's own terms (`seon.repl/located`)., but only prints them to stderr when the environment variable JOLT_CHECK is set (host/chez/compile-eval.ss); stderr reaches an agent only after its death, so an eval never shows them. Our REPL already holds each analyzed node: `seon.repl/wrap-record` binds ``jolt.analyzer/*unit-tap*`` to `seon.program/tap`, which is called with [form node], and `seon.repl/response` builds a response's `:warnings` from keys of its answers (:instrumented, :not-program, :shadowed), each entry `{:seon.repl/finding K ...}`. The nodes reach the wrap's around-reply as `(:nodes l)`, also for a form that threw, so `(first 42)`, which answers only its :ex today, can answer both. `(eval-in \"(first 42)\")` in seon.repl-test goes through this path. Correct: a small step of the same wrap calls `check-form` on each node the tap holds and puts its findings on the answer for `seon.repl/response` to add to `:warnings` as data (no environment variable, nothing refused: it records, docs/processes.md#an-agent-is-never-gated), rendered with the item. Measured silent on all of ``src`` (3,397 forms, 0 findings: docs/errors.md#an-eval-errors-diagnostics); not measured yet: the cost per form, owed as a cost proof of work done. Gate: the owner's go, since it adds content to an eval item (AGENTS §3 root's gate, 4). Deletes nothing; it replaces the idea of setting JOLT_CHECK. Jolt's JOLT_DIAG (the CLI's uncaught-error report), JOLT_DEBUG (build and cache diagnostics) and its nREPL server are deliberately unused: docs/errors.md#an-eval-errors-diagnostics."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-provably-wrong-call-is-a-warning-on-its-item seon.repl-test/a-checked-form-costs-its-nodes seon.repl-test/our-own-source-loads-with-no-type-warnings}}
 {:seon.task/name "records-keep-the-exact-value"
  :seon.task/plan "Storage keeps the exact value; renders budget it (a clipping function: docs/render.md#no-clipping). Left in src: `seon.system/end-agent` records a dead agent's :stderr as its last :seon.config.root/death-stderr-bytes though the file (`seon.agent/stderr-path`) is kept beside the store. Gone already: `seon.agent/error-map`'s message-chars cut, seon.model's 300-char HTTP error body, seon.learn's 80-char form and seon.agent-proc's 200-char form in :last-op and :running. Correct: each record holds the whole value or a reference to it (the stderr file's path and size); the context and the dashboard render them under their budgets through the one printer. Deletes root's use of `seon.lib/tail-bytes` in `seon.system/end-agent` and :seon.config.root/death-stderr-bytes as seon.system reads it (max-assertions is gone: every failing assertion is kept). `seon.lib/tail-bytes` itself stays: `seon.watch`, `seon.vector` and ``seon.fx/stall-evidence`` read it, and seon.watch reads the same key, for a watched process's death, which this plan does not decide. What shows the bug (ran 2026-11-06 at a root): `(seon.lib/tail-bytes path 2048)` of a 5,000-byte file answers a 2,038-character string (a cut note included), which is what `seon.system/end-agent` records as :stderr. Of the three named proofs only `seon.problems-test/a-red-assertion-is-persisted` exists, and it passes before any change (62 of 62 of seon.problems-test green on a fresh results dir): it checks that every failing assertion of a test is kept, which is the max-assertions part already gone, and reads no stderr. ``seon.agent-test/an-exception-message-is-recorded-whole`` and ``seon.problems-test/a-death-record-points-at-the-whole-stderr`` are not written."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-exception-message-is-recorded-whole
                      seon.problems-test/a-red-assertion-is-persisted
                      seon.problems-test/a-death-record-points-at-the-whole-stderr}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-repl-fallback-keeps-its-forms-position"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by batch 87's review (the REPL's names in every namespace, seon.agent/repl-name over Jolt's jolt.host/*resolve-unresolved*). Follow-ups in Jolt, on a jolt/<topic> branch of seantempesta/jolt; each ran 2026-11-06 in an agent. (1) jolt.analyzer/analyze-list*'s fallback arm analyzes (cons fallback (rest items)) without the form's metadata, which is still so in reference-code/jolt, but no lost position was seen: `(source 1 2 3 4)` on line 4 of a request answers :clojure.error/line 4 :column 1, and `(apropos (no-such-thing 1))` on line 3 answers its inner position. It needs an input that shows a lost position, or it is noise. (2) fallback-head calls resolve-global on every call head nothing shadows while the hook is bound, a second resolve of the head; reuse the resolution the macro arm makes, or measure it (jolt's analyzealloc gate, an agent's eval at n <= 100) and record that it is noise. Not measured. (3) A file loaded at the REPL takes the hook: `(let [] (load-file \"p.clj\"))` of a file holding `(def y (grep \"a\"))`, a name that file does not refer, answers nil, where Clojure throws. The unit corpus covers the hook under eval but not a load. `require` goes through jolt.loader, whose unbinding the original review doubted; not run, since it needs the file on the source path. The top-level `(load-file \"p\")` is another path, `seon.repl/eval-code`'s forms, each a top-level form of the request. The gates run as for any pin (docs/merging.md)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-file-loaded-at-the-repl-resolves-as-written}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-runs-in-one-mode"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime (was Optimization). Found by the guard audit of 2026-10-25 (docs/failure-points.md, S3-S6) and the blocking-calls audit (item 2). Read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521 [M]: an agent's body runs on the main carrier on every OS (seon.repl/run! calls jolt.host/main-carrier-run!). What is left of two modes: seon.sock/recv! picks a parking read or the blocking recvmsg arm by (and (jolt.host/main-carrier?) (jolt.host/fiber?)) (src/seon/sock.clj:197), and seon.intr registers signal wakes only on the main carrier (src/seon/intr.clj:87, :102). The agent's socket reader is an a/io-thread (seon.agent/intake, src/seon/agent.clj:71), a plain thread, so the blocking arm has a caller on every OS. The agent holds two more threads that a fiber does: seon.repl/serve-item! starts two a/io-threads per granted item though its docstring says a fiber (src/seon/repl.clj:1351-1361), and each test child's exit watch is an io-thread parked on jolt.io-poller/exit-chan, already a channel (agent.clj:1357). The question this task held is answered (deps, 2026-11-14): Jolt sockets park on the poller and are collect-safe, so the reader can move onto the carrier. Design (the Flow standard's edge rule: a blocking read at an edge is one reader; a read that parks needs no thread): the intake reader is a fiber on the main carrier parked in recv!'s parking arm, putting onto the :transport proc's in-port; serve-item!'s run and answer are go blocks; the exit watch is the tests proc's in-port fed by exit-chan. Then recv!'s blocking arm and the main-carrier? tests in sock and intr have no caller and go: one mode. Linux needs jolt.io-poller/the-io-poller-wakes-on-signals-on-linux first; this machine cannot run Linux, so its proof waits on that task. Deletes: recv!'s blocking arm, the main-carrier? branches (sock.clj:197, intr.clj:87, :102), three io-thread sites. Estimate: no thread per item or child: an agent running 3 granted items holds the same thread count as an idle one; a parked read costs one poller registration (~1k ticks). Proofs, red first: seon.agent-test/an-agent-running-items-holds-no-thread-per-item (count active threads, lib/active-threads, idle and with 3 items: equal; red today: +6); the design guard seon.check-test/a-thread-outside-a-proc-the-flow-primer-does-not-name-is-refused (the Flow standard's, seon.step/every-seon-proc-follows-one-flow-standard-with-a-runnable-example-each; red today on these three sites and seon.server's per-connection threads)."
  :seon.task/kind :work :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agent-running-items-holds-no-thread-per-item seon.check-test/a-thread-outside-a-proc-the-flow-primer-does-not-name-is-refused}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-docstring-redefinition-is-a-change"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction (root's end-to-end reuse measurement, 2026-10-27, on .worktrees/reuse-end-to-end's root at main 6b34178937): an outside agent evaluated `(in-ns 'seon.render.ui) (def utc-offset-ms \"Measured: Jolt has no time zones ...\" (let ...)) (in-ns 'user)`, the shipped form with only its docstring changed. The eval answered no :recorded (a defn changed in its body answers :recorded [seon.merge/divergence]), `(my.branch/changes)` did not list it, and the program digest did not move, so no test's digest moved and the service answered from its memo. Correct: a docstring is part of what a var is (help, the wiki's citations, the page), so a redefinition that changes only it is recorded as a change of the var, and a test whose reach includes the var takes the new form's digest. Ran 2026-11-06 in an agent. A defn's docstring-only change is recorded (`:recorded [seon.lib/r2]`, `:redefined`, in `(my.branch/changes)` under :changed). A def's is not when its value is identical: `(in-ns 'seon.print) (def default-budget \"a doc\" (:reply budgets))` answers no :recorded and is not under :changed; `(def default-budget (+ 8000 8000))`, a new body with the same value, the same; `(def default-budget 16001)` is recorded. Cause: `seon.program/set-vars` keeps a var only when its root changed while the form ran, `(not (identical? before root))`, and a re-evaluated def of a small number or keyword leaves the identical root, so no account is written for its new text. A map value is a new object and is recorded: at a root, on the nodes of `seon.program/tap`, `(def v 16000)` then `(def v \"a doc\" 16000)` answers `[]` from `set-vars`, and `(def m {:a 1})` then `(def m \"doc\" {:a 1})` answers `[m]`. So it is a gap for a def of an identical value whatever changed in its text, not only its docstring. Exact input for the proof, which fails on main: those two `(def v ...)` forms, each evaluated under `(binding [jolt.analyzer/*unit-tap* (seon.program/tap acc)] ...)`; `eval-in` is not agent mode and records nothing, so `:recorded` needs an agent. Owner: `seon.program/set-vars`, what it decides from."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-docstring-only-redefinition-is-recorded}}
 {:seon.task/name "print-length-is-the-sessions"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-30 (the optimization session, at root's REPL): (set! *print-length* 5) answers \"Can't change/establish root binding of: *print-length* with set\", and the printer shows a response with *print-length* and *print-level* nil (seon.print.emit binds them off). In clojure.main/repl and nREPL each session binds *print-length*, *print-level* and *print-meta* (as it binds *1 *2 *3 *e), so set! works and the value is printed under them: the well-known way a Clojure programmer asks for a smaller answer (docs/agent.md#small-answers). Seon's REPL binds *1 *2 *3 *e from :repl-vars (seon.repl's exec!) and not these. Correct: the session's *print-length* and *print-level* are kept beside :repl-vars, bound around each eval as clojure.main does, and the response's value is shown under them by the one printer (a collection past *print-length* shows its first items and the form that reads the rest by the handle, as any cut does); unset, nothing changes (an agent's own results are never cut unless it asks). Ran 2026-11-06 in an agent: `(set! *print-length* 5)` answers the same error. What this has to reach: a response is not printed in the eval. It is recorded as data and printed from the record by `seon.repl/text-of` and `seon.ctx.wire/tool-text`, when the request is assembled for a model, so a session's value at eval time has to travel with the record or the response. Which of the two is a design question this plan leaves open. Deletes nothing; replaces no mechanism (the printer's cut is the one cut)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/set-print-length-limits-the-sessions-answers}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-evals-failure-is-its-ex"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit read at c200a44aa9: \"make sure all of this fixes are applied and the docs are updated\"; by docs/merging.md#a-landing-message-carries-its-audit its report says what allowed the class, why the change makes it impossible, and which siblings it checked. What exists: a failed call is either a thrown :ex or an error map returned as :value, recognised by contract/error? (any map with a keyword :seon.error/type); af8b9c2af5 made seon.turn stop a reply's later calls on either, and 0a58e8aa31 then had to move grep's hint out of a value. Ran 2026-11-06 in an agent: `{:seon.error/type :plans/probe :seon.error/message \"a probe\"}` answers :value, no :ex. Readers that read only :ex, so an error value reads as success: seon.turn's model call :errors and its log's :error, `seon.render.ui/evaluation`'s err? (the cross and the red border), seon.reconcile. :ex is typed `:seon.error/base` in `:seon.repl/answer`, though `seon.render.ui/evaluation` still tests map? and falls back to the whole :ex. The change: in :seon.eval/response (seon.core.edn) :ex is the one failure member, typed as the error schema, never a string; seon.repl/eval-string, the one producer, answers a form whose value is an error map as :ex; every reader reads :ex alone, and the arm that reads `(contract/error? (:value r))` in `seon.repl/again-to` goes (`run-calls` has none now). A tool that advises without failing answers a value of a declared schema (grep's :my.agent/grep-none), as the rule. Docs: docs/agent.md#the-repl and docs/render.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-error-value-is-the-evals-failure}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-responses-contract-does-not-turn-success-into-an-error"
  :seon.task/plan "What was seen, in the lab, 2026-11-04: an eval of a form holding a doseq of my.schema/register! then defns came back as an error from seon.repl/response failing its own output contract (:malli.core/invalid-output), while the forms took effect. So an agent is told its work failed when it succeeded. The lab's file is gone from claude_scratch. Ran 2026-11-06 in an agent: registering new keys and a defn in one eval answers normally, and the class shows when the registered key is one seon.repl's own contract reads: `(doseq [k [:seon.wire/msg]] (my.schema/register! agent k '[:map [:plans-never :int]]))` then a defn answers `:seon.instrument/contract-error` from `seon.repl/response`, :malli.core/invalid-input, and every later eval of that agent, `(+ 1 1)` included, answers the same, while its forms ran. Cause: Seon's own contracts validate under the branch's schema registry as the agent left it. What should be true: an eval's answer says what its forms did, whatever the agent registered, and the agent can read why and put the key back. Not seen again: the :malli.core/invalid-output form of it, which would be a registered key under :seon.repl/answer. The same class as seon.repl/a-definition-that-breaks-seons-next-step-is-undone: an agent's change to what Seon's own code runs on."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-eval-that-registers-a-schema-answers-what-it-did}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-definition-that-breaks-seons-next-step-is-undone"
  :seon.task/plan "REDESIGNED 2026-11-16 (owner's ruling: rails, never guards; agreed context and runtime): seon.repl/undo-definition! (repl.clj:373, called at :527) undoes an agent's def that broke Seon's own recording: a guard. The constraint that replaces it is plan Stage 0.6: Seon's machinery (the turn loop, the recording, an eval's answer) runs from the program's held reference, never from the REPL namespace's live vars, so no agent's redefinition can cut the floor. Runtime builds that half; context then deletes undo-definition!, :seon.repl/definition-undone and their uses. Proof, red first: seon.repl-test/a-redefinition-of-the-answer-leaves-the-next-eval-answered (an agent redefines seon.repl/response; its next eval is answered and recorded). Needs runtime's half. The text below is the earlier plan, kept for its evidence.\n\nFound in the live lab, 2026-11-06: an agent working in seon.program ran (def form-of (fn [v] ...)) as a scratch name, every later write threw inside `seon.program/record-tx`, and the run ended on two turns in a row that threw. `seon.repl/undo-definition!` takes out what the recording itself calls. What is left of the class: a definition that records fine and then breaks what a turn does next (a status, a draw, a request), since an agent's process runs Seon's code through the same vars it can redefine. The message tells it (seon.turn/turn-error!), but when the request can't be drawn it can't be read, and two such turns end the run. Ran 2026-11-06 outside a turn, through bin/mcp: `(in-ns 'seon.ctx.status) (defn verdicts {:malli/schema [:=> [:cat] :int]} [] 1)` is recorded, its next evals answer, and the page shows `:seon.render/component-defect` for seon.render.ui/status saying the arity. A model's turn was not run here. Correct: the run goes on with the agent told, by undoing the reply's definitions (on the live var and on the branch, retracting what was recorded) when a turn throws before its model call. Not: direct linking of Seon's code, which would stop an agent's change to Seon from running in its own process, and which `docs/source.md` relies on. Proofs: an agent that redefines a helper of seon.ctx.status/status with another arity reads why on its next turn and finishes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-redefinition-of-the-answer-leaves-the-next-eval-answered}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-bad-edit-to-the-repl-leaves-a-way-back"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: held by the owner since 2026-11-07, not yet released for work. Reproduced 2026-11-07 on a scratch root. In a fresh agent, evaluate `(do (in-ns 'seon.repl) (clojure.core/defn response [& _] (throw (ex-info \"a bad edit\" {}))) (in-ns 'user) :edited)`. Every later eval, `(+ 2 2)` included, answers `{:threw \"a bad edit\"}`. An undo, `(ns-unmap 'seon.repl 'response)`, did run, since the error becomes `unbound fn`, but no eval answers again, so an agent spends its last turns unable to act (a real-model run, 2026-11-07). Correct: after that edit an eval still answers its value or an error saying which definition broke the REPL and the form that restores it from main. Proof: the edit above, then `(+ 2 2)` answers 4 or that error, and the restore form makes `(+ 3 3)` answer 6. Out of scope: refusing such an edit, since an agent's REPL stays free. Worked from a worktree, as it changes what's running."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-bad-edit-to-the-repl-leaves-a-way-back}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-form-stopped-by-out-of-memory-lets-go-of-what-it-allocated"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime (handed to Optimization, owner 2026-11-08: \"record the details and hand it to optimization\"). Found by Web's landing (2026-11-08, bin/check robust, intermittent: red once at load 40+): agent a2's r9, an allocation past its heap bound, answered OutOfMemoryError as its :ex, as expected; then r11's (+ kept 1) on the same agent answered OutOfMemoryError too: live 1,436,315,672 bytes against the ceiling 1,435,234,416, the top of the heap 1,023,112,336 bytes in 63,944,521 objects; the failing run's first error was \"GC overhead limit exceeded\" after 75 s. So r9's allocation stayed reachable after its form was stopped.\n\nMeasured, 2026-11-08 [M]: (1) plain Jolt keeps nothing: a caught GC-overhead error held in a var grew live memory by 0 MB at JOLT_MAX_HEAP 300m and 400m. (2) In an agent, a fresh agent is 638-639 MB live after a full collection and 1,635-1,657 MB after a top-level (count (vec (range 300000000))) fails on GC overhead, every time; the extra is 65,330,432 pairs, 996 MB (jolt.host/heap-histogram), all in collectable generation 4, still reachable with Jolt's tight GC mode off. (3) The same form nested in one eval keeps nothing, through seon.repl/eval-string (so exec!, error-kept, error-data, the record and the reply are not it), seon.agent/on-the-wire, seon.agent/commit! and seon.intr/run with tick and byte budgets. (4) Nothing later frees it. (5) Chez's make-object-finder from its default root or the oblist finds no path: the holder is a Jolt runtime-library global, another thread's stack, or a locked object. (6) The ceiling path is clean (Context's helper: 4 rounds back to ~705-722 MB); Jolt raises the ceiling error only after a full collection (rt.ss gc-enforce-ceiling!). (7) Jolt's two out-of-memory errors differ (rt.ss jolt-gc-raise-pending!, ~994-1003): the ceiling error's capture is trimmed and pins nothing; the GC-overhead error, (error 'jolt msg), is untrimmed and held keeps the live set at the throw (+25 MB at that size). Why intermittent: the overhead limit is a share of time collecting, so it fires under load. Ruled out: @seon.agent/state and *e to depth 6, error-kept, :repl-vars, the reply path. Not yet read: the agent's top-level path only, so the holder is the :repl proc's step state or a channel buffer, the :transport proc, root's side (c/submit!'s promise and record), a fiber, the io-poller, or a Datahike transaction in flight (the overhead error once surfaced in datahike.datom/val-at-datom).\n\nWhat correct looks like: a form stopped by OutOfMemoryError leaves nothing it allocated reachable from the agent (*1/*e, the response, the error's data, a held frame), so the next form runs within its heap; an error keeps data, never a Throwable whose frames pin the system (docs/errors.md#an-error-keeps-data). Next observation, no fix before it: run the form through agent-step on a Flow fiber under the :transport proc in plain Jolt with no root, then object-finder rooted at Jolt's runtime globals and each carrier's continuation; the holder named decides the fix. Context's proposed Jolt change (raise the overhead error as the ceiling error is raised, capture trimmed) is NOT the fix by (1); if the holder is the untrimmed capture on the agent's path, it is a jolt/<topic> branch with Jolt's gates and the owner's go. If the cause is a collection not yet run, the next form's start makes room, never a sleep. Estimate: the proof allocates past a 300 MB ceiling once (~3e9 ticks [H]), then one full collection. No law fits a reachability class; the proof is its guard. Proof, red first: seon.repl-test/a-form-stopped-by-out-of-memory-leaves-the-heap-free (a test env that allocates past a small ceiling, forces the overhead path with JOLT_GC_TIME_LIMIT and JOLT_GC_HEAP_FREE_LIMIT, then runs (+ 1 1) with live heap back to baseline within a bound)."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-form-stopped-by-out-of-memory-leaves-the-heap-free}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-draws-its-own-page"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: held until stage 4 (the user agent, its consumer), which Web lands with seon.render/the-user-agent-renders-the-users-view. Owner, 2026-11-09: \"I'd like it if the agents process itself could render ... Channels over flow?\". Read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521 [M]: root draws an agent's page two ways, seon.render.proc's agent-view (src/seon/render/proc.clj:522): it asks the agent's process (ask-parts, :456, answered by seon.repl/render-ctx, src/seon/repl.clj:1271, as the :render op inside the :repl proc's agent-step, :1305-1324), or, with no process, none that answered or one busy with its run, draws from the branch itself (seon.ctx/build and seon.render/draw); a run is one :eval held by :repl until it ends, so a busy agent answers no draw. The built work is NOT on any branch: jolt/hiccup is gone and its head afacea816f is reachable from no ref (git branch --list, git merge-base) [M]: it must be kept (a ref on jolt/runtime) or it is lost at the next gc. Measured 2026-11-09 [M]: a model call parks the :repl fiber; a warm draw in the agent is 5.5-6.1 ms, 1.4M ticks, 6.6-7.4 MB for 28-37 units; a cold draw is 145-200 ms and ~200 MB; on afacea816f a one-component change cost 22 contracted calls, 24k ticks, ~60 KB at 15, 108 and 1,000 evals (constant); a new model turn is linear in turns (owed to 2a).\n\nDesign, one producer (runtime builds the agent side; Web the root side): the agent's process draws its own page as a Flow proc of its own graph (seon.repl/serve!: :transport, :repl, :tests, and :render), fed by :transport on its own in-port, so it answers while :repl parks (a model call, I/O). A draw is read-only: it reads the db at ctx's own point ((:t @ctx), commit-as-db), the opened evals come in the ask, and it commits nothing; :repl is the one flusher of the walk. While a page watches, each commit puts its t on a sliding(1) channel (one stream whose newest subsumes the rest), so a burst coalesces, and only the parts that moved are pushed ({:parts [{:id :rev :hiccup}]}); with no watcher it does nothing. Root (Web): places the parts, keeps the last draw keyed (agent key, branch commit), deletes its own branch draw. Under the one derivation (docs/plan.md item 5) the draw is the agent's render reaction over its branch. Limit: an eval that computes without parking holds the carrier. Deletes: root's branch draw in agent-view and the cold draw. Estimate: a moved commit costs the parts it moved, constant in page size (24k ticks measured). Proofs, red first: seon.repl-test/a-busy-agent-answers-a-draw-during-a-model-call; seon.repl-test/a-draw-writes-nothing-to-the-branch; seon.repl-test/the-render-points-kept-are-those-an-asker-can-name (on afacea816f); seon.render.proc-test/an-agents-page-has-one-producer (the design guard: agent-view names no branch draw; red today).\n\nweb-2, 2026-11-14: commit afacea816f (render points kept while an asker can name them) is reachable from no branch since jolt/hiccup went, and is kept by the tag keep/web-afacea816f. Re-judge this task before building it [H: largely superseded]: the agent's walk is a value (W-4a, c19d3f7e81), root's kept walks are values (the reaction core, 29e96b1f60), and a branch head becomes a Store a render derefs (seon.render.proc/a-branch-head-is-a-store-a-render-derefs)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render.proc-test/an-agents-page-has-one-producer seon.repl-test/a-busy-agent-answers-a-draw-during-a-model-call seon.repl-test/a-draw-writes-nothing-to-the-branch seon.repl-test/the-render-points-kept-are-those-an-asker-can-name}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-eval-that-never-returns-gives-the-model-its-turn-back"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by curated pair 5 (Context, 2026-11-10; evidence in hang-pair5-2026-11-10: the agent's fiber dump by kill -INFO, sample, lsof). Reproduction, one eval in user: a chan over a reified clojure.core.async.impl.protocols/Buffer whose full? always answers false, then (dotimes [i 5] (a/>!! c i)) and two <!!. The eval's fiber parked in jolt-fiber->!* inside jolt-run-interruptible* under seon.repl/eval-code; the agent's REPL never answered again (over 15 min, ended by stopping its root). Every wait ends with its value or a typed error (docs/errors.md#nothing-crashes-and-nothing-hangs); a parked fiber burns no ticks, so the eval's tick budget never fires, and the turn gives the model no next call until the eval answers. Read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521 [M]: still so; an eval request carries no bound, and root's only watch is its :stall-check poll (src/seon/system.clj:939) with the agent proc's :stall? (src/seon/agent_proc.clj:122), which records a stall and ends nothing. Three paths, in order of cost: (1) The cheaper read first (deps): a put on a buffer whose full? is always false should never park; that it parked says jolt.async's put decides by something else of the reified Buffer (count, add!*'s answer), which may be the defect with no detector needed; read jolt.async's put in its source before anything else. (2) The backstop, runtime's deadline slice, no watchdog: each request's own bound (:seon.bound/bound on the request, default :seon.config.agent/stall-ms), armed by seon.step/put-after! when the agent proc takes it; past it root interrupts the eval (jolt.host/interrupt! on its interrupt, as the page's stop does) and the turn gets the typed :seon.bound/reached with the fiber dump as evidence (tasks seon.agent-proc/a-stalled-eval-ends-with-its-error and seon.step/every-wait-past-a-bound-is-a-flow-message-from-the-one-timer); first checked: that jolt-fiber-interrupt! reaches a fiber parked on a channel's alt-handler (the dump says wait state none). (3) The exact detector (Jolt, deps): a fiber parked on a channel nothing live holds but its own waiter is woken with a typed error naming the channel, known at a collection through a weak reference from the wait record (Go's leak detection); Jolt already does it for the whole program (jolt-main-carrier-deliver-deadlock!), which this case does not trip. Deletes: with (2), root's :stall-check poll and :stall? (the deadline slice's deletion). Estimate: one put-after! per eval request (~1k ticks); an interrupt is one signal. Proofs, red first: seon.repl-test/eval-parks-channel-nothing-live-holds-reified-never (the reproduction answers, its value or a typed error, and the agent's next eval runs); seon.agent-test/a-stalled-eval-is-answered-with-its-error (path 2's); the design guard seon.check-test/an-unbounded-wait-the-flow-primer-does-not-name-is-refused (the Flow standard's)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"jolt.async/a-parked-fiber-nothing-can-wake-is-told"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-stalled-eval-is-answered-with-its-error seon.check-test/an-unbounded-wait-the-flow-primer-does-not-name-is-refused seon.repl-test/eval-parks-channel-nothing-live-holds-reified-never}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-file-the-branch-cannot-hold-is-refused-before-it-is-written"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: part (1) needs the owner's ruling \"any file moves by branch\" (system.edn as a branch kind); part (2), refusing an edit of a path no branch holds before any write, needs no ruling and could be its own KEEP task (an edit! by checkout path of a file no branch holds answers an error and writes no file). Found by curated pair 5 (2026-11-10): an agent edited resources/seon/system.edn by its checkout's absolute path; seon.repl's :path place wrote it to disk (\"Wrote to disk at ...: no branch holds this, and no merge carries it\"), into the root's own checkout, and done reported it as :checkout-not-merged (the diff is kept in the managing session's notes, pair5-system-edn-written-into-checkout.diff). system.edn is read from the binary's tree at boot (seon.graph, seon.main) and digested as :seon.env/system (seon.fx), but is no file kind a branch holds (held-file's allow-list, seon.config/file-kind), so a task whose change is a proc's declaration cannot be done on a branch. Correct: (1) system.edn joins the kinds a branch holds, its text a fact like a doc's, read by the root at boot from main; (2) until then, an edit of a path no branch holds is refused before any write, naming why, never written into a checkout another process runs from. Owner's ruling wanted for (1): \"any file moves by branch\"."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "an-uncited-public-defn-is-told-at-its-eval"
  :seon.task/plan "Loop R (Point 2029, row R5): an agent learned that a public var's docstring cites a docs/ section only when the merge check refused it, 2 turns. The rule is shown where the agent acts: the eval that defines a public var whose docstring cites no docs/ section answers so in its result, from the law's own data (the same reader the merge check uses, seon.problems' uncited-function class), as the eval already says an uncontracted function will be refused. Proof: an eval of (defn f \"no cite\" [x] x) in an agent namespace answers the cite rule and its form; one with a cite answers nothing of it. State 2026-11-14 ~14:20 (context): the work is on branch :task-seon-repl-an-uncited-public-defn-is-told-at-its-eval-r536881861 (the task's :seon.task/branch) (fresh from main 1916c1b31c, agent m4740bf0bbdb183c, outside, now ended), as five definitions: seon.repl's ns form with [seon.problems :as problems] added to main's, seon.repl/uncited (new, private), seon.repl/wrap-instrument (main's body plus the uncited finding), seon.repl/response (main's plus the :uncited-definition warning), and the proof seon.repl-test/an-uncited-public-defn-is-told-at-its-eval. (prove) there was clean: 65 of 65 in-process green, the proof among them, 253 process tests left to the gate. seon_done then waited 24 min with no gate running: the gate's first test child hung in Chez's fork_alone (deps' fork fix) and the judge's wait has no bound (tests' fix); evidence runs/evidence/gate-stall-1346. Next: once both land, seon_attach the task (not fresh) and seon_done it, watched; report the verdict to root. Earlier branches (c26fb0fd, nae67b62) are stale, not to be merged."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-uncited-public-defn-is-told-at-its-eval}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-form-tried-at-the-repl-is-not-program-until-said"
  :seon.task/plan "Found twice, 2026-11-11, working two tasks as an agent: a throwaway definition evaluated to see what something answers is recorded as program in the namespace the agent stands in (the answer says :recorded with the name, nothing more). One, a macro, is on its task's branch now and would have merged with the task. (ns-unmap ...) takes one back and the answer says :unmapped; what it leaves in the store, read on a branch: the var's entity with its name and no form, and the form that defined it, no longer live. Whether a merge carries that residue is not read. What exists: the recorder writes every definition an eval makes (docs/model.md#one-tool-eval); a require at the REPL is already told apart and warned as not program. Plan, to be tried at a REPL first: either a place to try, a namespace the merge never carries, named in the first message; or the :recorded line says what it means in words (this is now part of your change; ns-unmap takes it back) and the status lists what the branch defines beyond what its task cites. The data question, Data model's: what marks a form as tried and not program; the existing :form/source says a form was read from a file and is not that. Proof, red first: a definition made where trying is done is absent from what the branch merges; one made in a program namespace is present."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-tried-definition-does-not-merge}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-unresolved-name-answers-the-nearest"
  :seon.task/plan "Loop D (2026-11-11, t3-t6, four turns of forty): the agent wrote (seon.db/q '[...] (seon.db/db)) to list tasks; seon.db/db and seon.db/q are no vars, the eval answered 'No such var', and it took two more evals of ns-resolve and ns-publics to find my.db/q and my.db/db, the agent's own. The doc macro already answers a name that is none with the nearest names (my.agent/no-var); an eval's unresolved symbol does not. The change: an eval's 'No such var' or 'Unable to resolve symbol' error carries the nearest names the same way (one function, my.agent/no-var's, read by the eval's error), so (seon.db/db) answers that my.db/db is the one. Proof: an eval of (seon.db/db) answers an error naming my.db/db. What loop 2 (2026-11-12, capped at 40 with the proof unwritten) found, so a run starts from it: (seon.db/db) throws IllegalArgumentException 'No such var: seon.db/db' at run time with no ex-data, so seon.agent/error-map has no :jolt.error/symbol to read; ```seon.repl/error-data``` is the one place an eval's exception becomes its error map, and seon.repl already requires my.agent; ```my.agent/no-var``` ranks only within the namespace the name gives, so it never reaches my.db/db; Jolt's suggestions-for is edit distance (threshold 1 to 3 by length, at most 3, ties alphabetical); scanning every :var/name cost 457 MB and 94M ticks, while the 152 namespace names read in 1 ms and a seek per namespace of [:var/name ns/name] in 10 ms total; :seon.error/base is a closed map, so a new key on an eval's error needs a schema entry. Write the proof first (my.test/eval-in asks the REPL in this process)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-unresolved-name-answers-the-nearest}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-relative-path-to-source-is-the-branchs"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime (an agent's process and its directory). Found 2026-11-11: an agent process runs with the standing system's cwd, the main tree, so seon.repl's :path place ((edit \"src/x.clj\" ...)) or a plain spit with a relative path wrote into the owner's working copy; two such files changed the standing binary's tree id and emptied every agent's test inputs. Loop 2 (2026-11-12, agent l7296ba17942b5b2): (seon.repl/edit! agent \"<checkout>/src/my/agent.clj\" old new) answered 'Wrote to disk ... no branch holds this, and no merge carries it'; writing, reverting and checking the file cost three of its forty evals. Read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521 [M]: scripts and the config file are the branch's already (seon.repl's :script and :config places, src/seon/repl.clj:1012-1020, through held-file); a namespace's file is told by namespace-file-note (the ns edit form) but its :path place still writes the disk, relative to the process cwd (repl.clj:976-992, canonical path of at, through seon.fx/write-file); only a test child changes its directory (its run dir, src/seon/agent.clj:1325); an agent keeps root's cwd. The cause: the process's directory is inherited, not set at its one start. Design: (1) an agent's working directory is its own, set once at its start (seon.agent/init!, as the test child's run dir is), so a plain spit or slurp with a relative path never names the checkout; (2) the place of a path the source index holds (seon.fx/source-file?, src/seon/fx.clj:172: program source, tests, docs, task files) is the branch's: a read answers the branch's text, an edit routes to the var, ns, doc, script or config place, and a write that would reach the disk there is refused naming the edit form; any other relative path resolves in the agent's own directory. Neither reads the checkout through the cwd. Deletes: the :path place's write of a source-index file. Estimate: one chdir at a start; one source-file? test per path (~1k ticks). Proofs, red first: seon.repl-test/a-relative-path-to-source-is-the-branchs (an agent's (edit \"src/<a program file>\" old new) changes the var on its branch and no file under the checkout; a spit to that path is refused naming the edit form); the design guard seon.agent-test/an-agents-working-directory-is-its-own (a fresh agent's user.dir is not the checkout; red today)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agents-working-directory-is-its-own seon.repl-test/a-relative-path-to-source-is-the-branchs}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-unmapped-var-leaves-nothing-in-the-program", :seon.task/plan "Found by context, 11-13, reading agent c26fb0fd087cd25e's run (seon.repl/an-uncited-public-defn-is-told-at-its-eval), turns 50-62: a scratch helper defined by mistake in my.agent, then removed with (ns-unmap 'my.agent 'forms-read), left its form entity (still :form/defines its var, its keyword :my.agent/eof naming an unschemed key) and its var entity with :var/name, only the :var/form link retracted. The program kept it, the laws flagged it, and the agent spent 13 turns finding and clearing the orphan. An unmap records the var's removal whole: its var entity and the forms that define only it leave the program (seon.repl's unmapped record, :unmapped in the answer), so what the branch merges is what the REPL holds. Proof: define a var with a keyword in an agent's namespace, unmap it: no form or var entity of it remains on the branch, and no law row names it.", :seon.task/kind :work, :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-unmapped-var-leaves-nothing-in-the-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-fixture-is-edited-at-its-place"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner's ruling (root, 2026-11-15): every edit goes through the branch and its merge, never a direct file write; context's plan seon.ctx/every-session-works-as-a-seon-agent-does lists the builders, and this one is tests'. test/seon/*.edn are an import kind (seon.source's file kinds) with no edit place, so a fixture can only be changed by writing its file. PRIOR WORK to read before code: seon.repl/edit! and the places it edits (a form, a schema entry, a task, a section), seon.source's file kinds and their :text (the import of a fixture file), the write-back that renders a kind's file. Build a :fixture place for seon.repl/edit! over the kind's own import and render, so an edit of a fixture is a branch write that merges and is written back, as a form's is. Queued after E (root)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/a-fixture-edited-at-its-place-merges-and-is-written-back}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.repl{edit! {:n 1, :order :constant, :ticks 5000000, :bytes 20000000}}
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.proofs/a-test-forks-from-the-standing-system-and-a-binary-test-reruns-only-for-its-binary"}}
 {:seon.task/name "every-agent-is-a-remote-flow-proc-in-roots-graph"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner's order (11-12, ~14:05-14:15, set with runtime; memory processes-through-one-fork-flow): \"a child is a jolt.async.remote.flow/proc in its parent's graph (remote-pair at the fork, flow/update!, :conns)\". Asked again 2026-11-15 (\"a full abstraction and a proper fix so flow processes can send data across channels directly in the same way jvm core.async flow works ... not a hacked together workaround\"); root: finish it, after messaging parts 2-4 land. Plan: notes/design/one-unit-spec.md (runtime's fork-one / flow-children items).\nBUILT [M, flow read 2026-11-15]: jolt.async.remote, a core.async channel whose other end is in another process, ordered, back-pressured, carrying a descriptor (reference-code/jolt/stdlib/jolt/async/remote.clj :487 open, :519 remote-pair); jolt.async.remote.flow/proc and fork! (remote/flow.clj:11,53), used by an agent's test children only (src/seon/agent.clj:1394); root's end of each agent's socket is a remote end feeding seon.agent-proc (src/seon/system.clj:415).\nNOT BUILT: the agent's own end is hand-made seon.sock frames (src/seon/agent.clj:44-55 send!/intake); its :transport proc parses them (seon.repl/transport-step); await-reply keeps a promise table in seon.agent/state (agent.clj:255, an atom); agents are forked by the fork server, not as remote procs; the fork server itself steps on seon.step/run-loop (fork_server.clj:371), a second step loop.\nTHE CHANGE: every agent a remote.flow proc in root's graph on one wire: the agent's side a remote end (fiber pump), its procs' ports joined to root's by :conns, its requests to root Flow messages answered by step/pend (no await-reply table); the fork server a proc in a Flow graph (run-loop deleted; deps' one per-step measure covers it). Messages BETWEEN agents stay facts on main (the store is the truth), so no sibling channels.\nPRIOR WORK to read before code: remote.clj, remote/flow.clj, seon.agent-proc, seon.repl/transport-step and agent-step, seon.agent/await-reply and its 9 callers, seon.fork-server, seon.step (pend, ask-edge, end-all). Plan item 9 (end-all deletes await-reply's waits) folds in.\nCOST: to measure today, before the plan, at an agent and at root: one round trip of a request today (frame encode, root's decode, answer) vs a remote end's put and take; one fork. Estimate: equal or less per message (one codec instead of two framings), no added work per fork.\nDeletes: seon.sock's frame read and write on the agent side; the :transport proc's parsing; await-reply's table in seon.agent/state (an atom slice: :stateful-outside-flow); seon.step/run-loop. No functionality removed.\nBUILDERS: deps the library half (remote.flow), flow the agent end, runtime the fork server; flow writes the plan with both.\nOrder: after seon.server/a-message-reaches-the-session-that-drives-its-agent parts 2-4 land.\nAPPROVED (root, 2026-11-15; plan notes flow-remote-procs-plan-2026-11-15.md): one wire for THREE links (agent<->root, fork server<->root, embed worker<->root, all hand-framed today; the wire is already byte-identical, so each side moves alone), core.async.flow's tools unchanged (public API and SPI match core.async v1.10.874-alpha3 [M]; Jolt's 12 extra ::flow/ keys audited as additive), +~200 ticks a message [M result/c1f322d4]. OWNERS: flow the agent link; runtime the fork server (run-loop and start-event-reader!'s thread deleted); context the embed worker (seon.vector); deps the Jolt interface (remote.flow/proc forwarding its child graph's ping, end-all at ::flow/closed, an ask-edge over a remote proc) and the ::flow/ key audit. Each link lands on its own, after messaging parts 2-4.\n(4) ask-edge is flow's: an agent's request to root and its answer carry the request id on the wire, and an ask the link drops ends with a typed error (seon.step/end-all at ::flow/closed). The library half is deps' task jolt.async/a-remote-proc-shows-its-child-and-ends-its-asks (jolt/deps 26a7494b1e), which needs this one: the child pushes its graph's view on change, the remote proc keeps the last, a live view is an ask over the link.\nAGREED (deps, 2026-11-15): ::flow/closed is the one end signal, read twice, no second watcher. deps' remote proc ends every pending ask on it; flow's reader of the same output removes the proc by update! and reaps the child (rf/fork! leaves waitpid to the caller). Seen at the REPL [M, flow-lab/e23_remote_flow_fork.clj]: after its child ended, the rf proxy proc stayed :running."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agent-is-a-remote-flow-proc-in-roots-graph seon.agent-test/an-agents-request-to-root-is-a-flow-message-answered-once}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.agent{send! {:n 1, :order :constant, :ticks 20000, :bytes 4000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-template-instructions-test-ends"
  :seon.task/plan "Root, 2026-11-16: seon.repl-test/the-template-instructions-and-the-help-page-run stalls, ending only at its bound. A test that ends at its bound waits on something that never comes (AGENTS.md section 3, docs/clocks.md#two-kinds-of-wait).\n\nFirst: capture the stalled test's waits (``seon.system/stall-state``'s :unanswered site, as a test's capture records it) and name what it blocks on; fix that wait at its cause. Never raise the bound.\n\nPRIOR WORK: the test itself (test/seon/repl_test.clj); stall-state.\n\nREAD (context, 2026-11-16) [M]: no stall is on record. Its verdicts under target/results: 68 green, one red (an attach before a build, 2026-11-15 16:46, env-mismatch); the last three green at 35.8M ticks each and 10.9, 23.2 and 35.0 s wall. The wall is ~300x the test's own ticks: the work is in the agent it forks (in-agent, attach), whose ticks this verdict does not count. NEXT: the stall's evidence when it next ends at its bound (bin/test keeps the capture), and the agent's own cost read beside the test's, so its estimate counts both processes. RED ON MAIN, READ (context and tests, 2026-11-16): green alone (36M ticks on main's tip, and on tests' branch), red only in a shared test root after other agent tests (tests' land 105447 and data's 105743, shard 3 each, after different tests), where every navigation form the help page names printed nothing, ls and lines included: an earlier test left the shared root's main, or the branch the next agent forks from, without the program's facts. The class fix is THE FOCUS's invariant that a test's writes are speculative (step 2: tests fork from the branch's warm process); until then lands leave this test out by name (root's order)."
