 {:seon.task/name "an-edit-that-breaks-the-agents-own-recording-is-undone", :seon.task/kind :work, :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-definition-that-breaks-the-next-recording-is-undone}, :seon.task/estimate #:seon.repl{serve! {:n 1, :order :constant, :ticks 1000000, :bytes 10000000}}, :seon.task/plan "Found by data in the first real seon_done (2026-11-15, agent b011087cf254a4fd, task seon.db/an-eval-responses-parts-are-its-schemas-keys) [M]: one reply of four seon.repl/edit! evals; the first turned seon.db/result-parts from a map into a defn-. seon.db/with-response and seon.db/response-of (which record and read every eval's answer) still read it as a map, so the reply died with :seon.agent/eval-error at seon.repl/serve!, \"Don't know how to create ISeq from: seon.db$result_parts\", and so did EVERY later eval, before it ran: an edit! repairing with-response could not run either. The agent could only be killed (root: seon.system/kill!) and the task re-attached :fresh. The rail seon.agent-test/a-definition-that-breaks-its-own-recording-is-undone covers a def whose evaluation throws in seon.program/record-tx; it misses a definition that evaluates fine and breaks the next recording. Cause to read first (PRIOR WORK: seon.repl's definition-undone path, seon.repl/serve!'s recording): the undo is judged at the definition's own eval, never at the recording that follows. Fix direction [H]: the recording of the eval that made a definition is the check; a recording that throws undoes that eval's definitions (as :seon.repl/definition-undone does) and answers the error. Also: edit! takes one var, so a change across several definitions (a list made a fn and its readers) has no single edit; the workaround that held was new names first, readers next, the old names unmapped last."}
